WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.756
A persuasive argument has three connected parts.

00:00:02.756 --> 00:00:05.397
The claim tells your audience what to believe.

00:00:05.397 --> 00:00:07.923
The warrant explains why that claim follows.

00:00:07.923 --> 00:00:10.334
The impact tells us why the issue matters.

00:00:10.334 --> 00:00:14.870
If you skip the warrant, you ask the audience to supply your reasoning for you.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.895
Suppose your claim is that a later school start improves learning.

00:00:19.895 --> 00:00:23.024
Do not jump straight from the policy to better grades.

00:00:23.024 --> 00:00:29.630
Explain how a later start could change sleep, how sleep could affect attention, and
what evidence tests each link.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:33.165
This is an example to research, not a verified factual claim.

00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:38.514
A statistic is useful only when it supports the step you are defending.

00:00:38.514 --> 00:00:40.910
Record the author, date, title, and link.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:45.000
Check whether the source establishes causation or only a relationship.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.565
End by explaining the consequence and why it deserves weight.

00:00:48.565 --> 00:00:50.610
Now build one argument of your own.
